body.proof_tex:
Fix \(P\in\mathcal M_{2,\mathrm{loc}}\), let
\[
 L=4r+1,
 \qquad a_r=2r+1,
 \qquad \ell=2r+1,
 \qquad M=N_{n,\ell}=n-4r-1>0. \tag{1}
\]
By the Janson-boundary proposition, \(h_i\) is measurable with respect to \(\sigma(U_{i-r},\ldots,U_{i+r})\), and it is centered.  Since the two innovation blocks at distance \(\ell=2r+1\) are disjoint, iid innovations imply
\[
 \mathbb E_P[h_i h_{i+\ell}]=0. \tag{2}
\]
Cross-wave identification, stationarity, and the buffered coupling give, for every \(|k|\le2r\),
\[
 \mathbb E_P\!\left[
  \frac1{N_{n,k}}\sum_{i\in J_{n,k}}
  Z_{n,i}^{(1)}Z_{n,i+k}^{(2)}
 \right]
 =\mathbb E_P[m_0m_k]
 =\tau_{\mathrm P}^2+\gamma_h(k). \tag{3}
\]
The same argument at lag \(\ell\), followed by (2), gives
\[
 \mathbb E_P\!\left[
  \frac1{N_{n,\ell}}\sum_{i\in J_{n,\ell}}
  Z_{n,i}^{(1)}Z_{n,i+\ell}^{(2)}
 \right]
 =\tau_{\mathrm P}^2. \tag{4}
\]
There are exactly \(L\) integers in \([-2r,2r]\).  Subtracting \(L\) copies of (4) from the sum of (3) over those lags proves
\[
 \mathbb E_P[\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}]
 =\sum_{k=-2r}^{2r}\gamma_h(k)
 =\Omega_{\mathrm{pop}}. \tag{5}
\]

We now derive the deviation bound from independent primitive coordinates.  Because the conditional law of each assignment wave given \(\mathcal F_U\) is the same constant product \(\operatorname{Bernoulli}(p)\) law and the waves are conditionally independent, for cylinder events \(A,B,C\),
\[
 P\{U\in A,D^{(1)}\in B,D^{(2)}\in C\}
 =P\{U\in A\}P\{D^{(1)}\in B\}P\{D^{(2)}\in C\}. \tag{6}
\]
Together with iid innovations, (6) shows that the \(3n\) finite coordinates
\[
 U_1,\ldots,U_n,
 \quad D_1^{(1)},\ldots,D_n^{(1)},
 \quad D_1^{(2)},\ldots,D_n^{(2)}
\]
are mutually independent.  The revised buffered-restriction definition constructs every retained exposure, outcome, and propensity coordinate from its common radius-\(r\) cylinder map.  Thus changing one environmental coordinate changes at most \(a_r\) retained scores in each wave, while changing one assignment coordinate changes at most \(a_r\) retained scores in its own wave.  The exact score envelope and buffered coupling give \(|Z_{n,i}^{(w)}|\le K\) almost surely.

For pointwise application of the cited bounded-differences lemma, choose the following harmless Borel version.  On a local innovation block for which either cylinder propensity is smaller than \(\varepsilon\), set the corresponding local score map equal to zero; elsewhere use the displayed IPW formula.  Exposure positivity says that this modification occurs on a null event for every retained site, so the resulting finite statistic equals \(\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}\) almost surely.  The modified score is bounded by \(K\) on every input block, not merely almost surely.  Consequently changing either or both factors changes a product by at most \(2K^2\).  We use this version in the coordinate-change calculation below.

At any fixed lag \(|k|\le2r\), one environmental coordinate affects at most \(2a_r\) products.  Since
\[
 N_{n,k}=n-2r-|k|\ge n-4r>M, \tag{7}
\]
the sum of the \(L\) short-lag averages changes by at most \(4a_rLK^2/(n-4r)\).  The lag-\(\ell\) average, after multiplication by \(L\), changes by at most \(4a_rLK^2/M\).  Hence the total environmental-coordinate oscillation is at most
\[
 4a_rLK^2\left\{\frac1{n-4r}+\frac1M\right\}
 \le \frac{8a_rLK^2}{M}. \tag{8}
\]
For one assignment coordinate only one wave's factor can change.  The identical count with \(a_r\), rather than \(2a_r\), affected products gives total oscillation at most
\[
 2a_rLK^2\left\{\frac1{n-4r}+\frac1M\right\}
 \le \frac{4a_rLK^2}{M}. \tag{9}
\]
Set \(c=a_rLK^2/M\).  Summing squared oscillations over the \(n\) environmental and \(2n\) assignment coordinates yields
\[
 n(8c)^2+2n(4c)^2=96nc^2. \tag{10}
\]
The cited McDiarmid bounded-differences lemma, first with arbitrarily enlarged strict bounds and then by letting the enlargement decrease to zero, now gives for every \(t>0\),
\[
 P\!\left\{
  \left|\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}
  -\mathbb E_P\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}\right|>t
 \right\}
 \le2\exp\!\left\{-\frac{t^2}{48nc^2}\right\}. \tag{11}
\]
Taking
\[
 t=\sqrt{48n}\,c\sqrt{\log(2/\delta)}
 =\frac{\sqrt{48n}(2r+1)(4r+1)K^2}{n-4r-1}
  \sqrt{\log(2/\delta)}
 =q_n^{\mathrm u}(\delta) \tag{12}
\]
in (11), and using (5), proves the asserted concentration inequality.

If \(\Omega_{\mathrm{pop}}=0\), rejection implies a deviation strictly larger than \(q_n^{\mathrm u}(\alpha)\), so (11) gives size at most \(\alpha\).  If
\[
 \Omega_{\mathrm{pop}}
 \ge q_n^{\mathrm u}(\alpha)+q_n^{\mathrm u}(\beta), \tag{13}
\]
nonrejection implies \(\Omega_{\mathrm{pop}}-\widehat\Omega_n^{\mathrm u}\ge q_n^{\mathrm u}(\beta)\).  For every \(\beta'\in(\beta,1)\), strict decrease of \(q_n^{\mathrm u}(\cdot)\) turns this into a deviation strictly exceeding \(q_n^{\mathrm u}(\beta')\); (11) bounds its probability by \(\beta'\).  Letting \(\beta'\downarrow\beta\) proves power at least \(1-\beta\).  Finally, for fixed \(r,B,\varepsilon,\alpha,\beta\), (12) is \(O(n^{-1/2})\), and (5) confirms that no sample-centering bias allowance is present.
